{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And kept My charge</b> — This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 26:5): \"My voice\" — when I tested him. \"My charge (משמרתי)\" — decrees for distancing, such as secondary prohibitions of incest (שניות לעריות) and Sabbath restrictions (שבות לשבת). \"My commandments (מצותי)\" — commandments that even had they not been written, it is logical that they would have been, such as robbery and bloodshed. \"My statutes (חקתי)\" — matters against which the evil impulse and the nations of the world object, such as eating pork and wearing sha'atnez, for which there is no reason except that the King decreed His statute upon His servants. \"My teachings (ותורתי)\" — to bring in the oral Torah, halakhah le-Moshe mi-Sinai. If so, all of this rests on the view that Abraham observed and kept the Torah before it was given.\n\nAnd thus they said (Genesis Rabbah 95:3) regarding the verse \"And Joseph gave them wagons\" (Genesis 45:21) — that he interpreted from it the passage of the broken-necked heifer (עגלה ערופה), that he was engaged in Torah just as his fathers were, and until now the Torah had not yet been given — yet behold it is written \"and kept My charge, My commandments, My statutes, and My teachings.\" And there they said that he kept even the precise refinements (דקדוקי) of Torah, and that he taught his sons, and so forth.\n\nOne must ask: if so, how did Jacob set up a massebah (Genesis 28:18), and marry two sisters — and according to our Rabbis' view (Genesis Rabbah 74:11), four? And Amram married his aunt (Exodus 6:20), and Moses our teacher set up twelve massebot (Exodus 24:4). And how is it possible that they would act with license in the Torah regarding what Abraham their father had forbidden upon himself, and HaShem had fixed a reward for it, and he would command his sons and his household after him to walk in his ways? And concerning Jacob they expounded (Genesis Rabbah 79:6) that he kept the Sabbath and established Sabbath boundaries (תחומין) — and perhaps this could be so regarding the Sabbath, because it is weighted equally against the entire Torah (Jerusalemite Berakhot 1:5), for it testifies to the work of Creation.\n\nOr perhaps we should say: \"My charge (משמרתי)\" — secondary incest prohibitions for the children of Noah; \"My commandments (מצותי)\" — robbery and bloodshed; \"My statutes (חקתי)\" — limb from a living animal and forbidden mixtures (כלאים) of crossbreeding animals and grafting trees; \"and My teachings (ותורתי)\" — laws (דינין) and the prohibitions of idolatry — for the children of Noah were commanded in all of these, and he was one who kept and did the will of his Creator, observing even refinements and stringencies in the commandments that applied to them. And as they mentioned: the idolatry of Abraham our father comprised four hundred chapters of law (Sanhedrin 14a [cf. Avodah Zarah 14a]), and they expounded \"a hundred gates\" that he measured out for tithes (Genesis Rabbah 64:6), for the Patriarchs were generous people who gave tithes to the poor or to the priests of YHWH, such as Shem and Eber and their disciples, as in the matter of \"and he was a priest of El Elyon\" (Genesis 14:18).\n\nWhat appears to me according to the view of our Rabbis is that Abraham our father learned the entire Torah through the holy spirit (רוח הקודש), and engaged in it and in the reasons for its commandments and its secrets, and kept it all like one who is not commanded yet acts, and his observance of it was in the Land only. And Jacob, outside the Land, married the sisters, and likewise Amram, because the commandments are the law of the God of the Land, even though we were warned with a bodily obligation in every place (Kiddushin 36b). And our Rabbis already hinted at this secret (Sifrei Devarim, parashah 43), and I shall remind you of it with the help of HaShem (Leviticus 18:25, Deuteronomy 11:18). And the massebah is a commandment that was renewed at a known time, as they expounded (in Sifrei) on \"which YHWH your God hates\" (Deuteronomy 16:22) — that He hated it after it had been beloved in the days of the Patriarchs. And concerning Joseph they expounded (Genesis Rabbah 92:4) that he kept the Sabbath even in Egypt, because it is weighted equally against all the commandments, since it is testimony to the renewal of the world, and he did so to teach his sons the faith of the Creation of the world, to remove from their hearts the intent of idolatry and the beliefs of the Egyptians — and this was their intention.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense (פשט), we may say that \"My charge (משמרתי)\" is the faith in divinity — that he believed in HaShem the One, and kept this charge in his heart, and distinguished himself by it from the worshippers of idolatry, and he proclaimed the name of YHWH to return many to His service. \"My commandments (מצותי)\" — everything that He commanded him in \"Go forth from your land,\" and the offering of his son, and the banishment of the maidservant and her son. \"My statutes (חקתי)\" — to walk in the ways of HaShem, to be gracious and merciful and to perform righteousness and justice, and to command his sons and his household in them. \"And My teachings (ותורתי)\" — circumcision upon himself, his sons, and his servants, and all the commandments of the children of Noah, which are Torah for them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:39.420206595Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}